Step 2: Change the ThemeGitHub Pages officially pre-installs a collection of standard themes:minima (clean blog default)cayman (bold header, modern landing page)slate (dark theme, code-oriented)architect (technical, framed structure)tactile (soft grid paper aesthetic)merlot (minimalist sidebar layout)dinky (compact layout)How to switch:Open _config.yml in your editor.Locate or add the theme: line:YAMLtheme: cayman
If using bundle exec jekyll serve, restart the server (Ctrl + C, then run it again) because changes to _config.yml require a server restart to take effect.Refresh http://localhost:4000 to view the new look.Step 3: Start Customizing Content and Navigation1. Site Metadata (_config.yml)Set global variables that Jekyll themes display in headers, footers, and page titles:YAMLtitle: Simonas
description: Visual Effects, 3D Post-Production, and Tech Notes
theme: minima

# If your repo is named "hompage" and NOT "username.github.io", set:
baseurl: "/hompage"
url: "https://<your-username>.github.io"
2. Add Navigation PagesAny Markdown file with YAML front matter becomes a page. Create about.md in the root folder:Markdown---
layout: page
title: About
permalink: /about/
---

## About Me

Write your bio, skills, or portfolio overview here.
Themes like minima will automatically pick up this page and place a link to /about/ in the top navigation bar.Step 4: Customize CSS and LayoutsJekyll allows you to override any file inside a theme without touching the gem files. The precedence order is simple: any file in your project folder overrides the theme's default file of the same name and path.1. Add Custom CSSCreate a custom stylesheet file at assets/css/style.scss:SCSS---
---
@import "{{ site.theme }}";

/* Add custom overrides below */
body {
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
}

a {
  color: #0066cc;
}
(The two lines of triple dashes --- at the top are mandatory—they tell Jekyll to compile the SCSS file).2. Override Layouts or PartialsIf you want to modify the header, footer, or HTML structure:Create a folder named _includes or _layouts in your project root.Add files such as _includes/header.html or _includes/footer.html to replace the theme's built-in header or footer with your own HTML.